1. Introduction: why state comes first
1.1 The dependency chain
State (A4)
 ├── VERIFY (A5): preconditions are predicates over state
 ├── Plans (A3): plans read and write state
 ├── Actions (A2): action effects are state changes
 ├── Invocation (A6): the context hash is a state projection
 ├── Memory (A7): log entries record state deltas
 └── Composition (A8): sub-task sequencing depends on state
Remove A4 and every other sub-spec becomes undefined.
1.2 Hypothesis H3
The Verifiable Agency Theorem names three hypotheses: H1 (finite action library, A2), H2 (finite plan library with bounded plans, A3) and H3. A4 supplies H3:
H3. The state space S and the input alphabet Σ are finite, and the transition function δ : S × Σ → S is a total, deterministic function.
A4 must deliver finiteness and determinism by constructing a finite representation of reality, not by hoping reality is finite.
1.3 Design principle
The state is not the world. The state is a finite, quantized, deterministic projection of the world, sufficient for the agent's declared task domain and no larger.
Safety-critical systems already work this way. A flight controller models airspeed, altitude, attitude and a bounded set of environmental inputs, quantized to the precision certified control requires. A4 generalizes that discipline. It also inherits the discipline's limit: the controller is only as good as its model, which §7 and §8 make explicit.
2. State schema
2.1 Definition 1 (State)
A state s ∈ S is a tuple:
s = ⟨ s_mode, s_ctx, s_world, s_mem, s_meta ⟩
Component
Description
Bound
s_mode
FSM state (IDLE, PLAN, EXECUTE, VERIFY, SAFE, …)
|M| states, M finite
s_ctx
Active tile context (which tile, which task)
|T| × |TaskID|
s_world
Quantized world model (sensor state)
Product of sensor domains
s_mem
Bounded window of recent log entries
At most W entries
s_meta
Metadata (cycle counter, flags)
Bounded integers
Every component is drawn from a finite domain. Their product is S. The mode set M contains a distinguished SAFE mode (§4.4, §6).
2.2 Proposition (finiteness)
|S| < ∞.
Proof. Each component has a finite domain: s_mode by A1 (finitely many FSM states), s_ctx by A6 (finite tile set, finite task id space), s_world by §3 (sensors are quantized), s_mem by A7 (window of at most W entries over a finite entry set), s_meta by bounded integer ranges. A finite Cartesian product of finite sets is finite. □
|S| is finite but may be very large. The proposition claims finiteness only. Verification does not enumerate S (§6.4).
2.3 The state is total
Every field is always defined. There is no null state and no partial state. When a sensor fails or its reading is unusable, that is a value in its domain (§3.4), not an absence.
This makes the state total. It does not make the agent omniscient. The agent can be wrong about the world, or unsure of it, and the model represents that as explicit values (FAULT, STALE, IMPLAUSIBLE). What cannot happen is an undefined state.
3. World model
3.1 Definition 2 (World model)
The world model s_world is a tuple of quantized sensor readings:
s_world = ⟨ r_1, r_2, …, r_n ⟩,   r_i ∈ D_i
where each D_i is a finite domain declared in the sensor's schema.
