3.3 Proposition (world finiteness)
|s_world| < ∞. Proof. Each D_i is finite by declaration, and the number of sensors n is finite and declared. A finite product of finite sets is finite. □
Design constraint: the sensor set is declared at design time. There is no dynamic sensor discovery.
3.4 Fault and plausibility handling
A reading enters the world model only through input conditioning (§4.2), which applies the schema's checks in a fixed order:
1.  Range and rate: a reading outside the declared range or rate-of-change becomes IMPLAUSIBLE.
2.  Staleness: a reading not refreshed within the declared window becomes STALE.
3.  Redundancy: where a redundancy group is declared, disagreement beyond a declared tolerance sets all members to IMPLAUSIBLE.
4.  Failure: a sensor reporting failure takes its fault_value.
Downstream logic treats these as ordinary values, so the same fault always produces the same state. Only detected faults take reserved values. A wrong reading that passes every check is a valid value, and that residual is named in §8.
4. Sensor interface
4.1 Definition 3 (Input alphabet)
Σ is the set of sampled reading vectors, one reading per sensor per cycle:
Σ = D_1 × D_2 × … × D_n
|Σ| = ∏ |D_i|
A transition is the pair (s, σ), so the change from one reading to the next is carried by s and σ together and needs no separate alphabet of transitions. This replaces the v1.0 bound ∏|Dᵢ|², which counted pairs and did not match the formal model.
4.2 Input conditioning
Raw sensor values become σ through a conditioning function declared per deployment:
σ_t = cond(raw_t, s_world at t-1)
conditioning is deterministic and total. It applies quantization, rate limits, hysteresis, relevance filters and the checks of §3.4. It uses exact integer arithmetic, so it is bit-identical across platforms.
4.3 Sampling discipline and reaction latency
Sensors are sampled at declared rates, synchronously with the FSM cycle. No asynchronous interrupt may change S. This removes race conditions and nondeterministic interleaving, so the transition is always s' = δ(s, σ) for the σ sampled that cycle.
The price is latency. For every hazard that needs a timed reaction, the deployment declares T_react and shows:
sensor_latency + T_cycle + AET  ≤  T_react
A hardware fail-safe path that never touches S (a watchdog, a hardwired stop, a power gate) is permitted. It is not governed by A4. The deployment declares it in its safety case.
4.4 Definition 4 (Transition)
δ : S × Σ → S,    s_{t+1} = δ(s_t, σ_t)
Property
Meaning
Deterministic
δ is a function, not a relation
Total
δ is defined for every (s, σ) in S × Σ
Bounded
Every transition completes within the FSM cycle budget
Guarded
A transition that would leave the invariant set I is not committed. The agent enters SAFE and emits 0x-UNSAFE-ACT
The guard is constructed as follows. Let step(s, σ) be the FSM step applied to s with its world replaced by σ, including every write mediated by VERIFY (§5.2). Let safe(s, σ) be s with mode set to SAFE and world set to σ. Then:
δ(s, σ) = step(s, σ)   if step(s, σ) ∈ I
          safe(s, σ)   otherwise
5. State access protocol
5.1 Read access
Tiles read state through a read-only projection. Each tile declares the fields it needs:
Tile_Read_Scope(tile_id) ⊆ { fields of S }
A tile cannot read outside its scope. This is the isolation property (A6) applied to state.
5.2 Write access
No tile writes state directly. A tile proposes a transition, and VERIFY (A5) checks it against declared preconditions, envelope bounds and the invariant set. Only after PASS does the transition commit.
propose_transition : S × Tile → (S → S)
5.3 The state log
Every committed transition is recorded in the append-only log (A7):
Entry = ⟨ timestamp, prev_state_hash, transition_id, next_state_hash, signature ⟩
The log is the agent's complete history. It is auditable, reproducible and signed. Transitions into SAFE are logged like any other.
5.4 Context hashing (for A6)
For tile invocation, the invoker provides:
ctx_hash = H(s_world_projection, s_ctx)
The invoked tile recomputes the hash and checks that it matches. This integrity check assumes H is collision-resistant (SHA-256 or a ternary-compatible equivalent). If H is broken, the check proves nothing, so the choice of H belongs in the deployment's declared trust base.
6. Invariants
6.1 Definition 5 (Invariant set)
Two kinds of condition must hold, and they are different in kind.
• Φ_int constrains the agent's own state: mode, context, memory window, metadata. The agent controls these, so δ can preserve them.
• Φ_world constrains the world-facing reading, for example "brake pressure is within safe bounds". The agent does not control the world, so δ cannot preserve Φ_world. It can only respond when it fails.
The invariant set is:
I = { s ∈ S : Φ_int(s)  and  ( Φ_world(s)  or  s_mode = SAFE ) }
So a reading that breaks Φ_world is allowed to occur, and the invariant says what the agent must do when it does: be in SAFE.
6.2 Requirements
s0 ∈ I                                    (initial states lie in I)
∀ s ∈ I, σ ∈ Σ :  δ(s, σ) ∈ I          (Preservation)
The guarded construction of §4.4 gives Preservation provided one local obligation holds:
Safe-state closure (SC). For every s ∈ I and σ ∈ Σ, Φ_int(safe(s, σ)) holds.
Proof of Preservation under SC. If step(s, σ) ∈ I, then δ(s, σ) = step(s, σ) ∈ I. Otherwise δ(s, σ) = safe(s, σ). That state has mode SAFE, so it lies in I exactly when Φ_int holds, which SC supplies. □
SC is a one-line check on the deployment: moving to SAFE and swapping in the new reading must not break an internal invariant.
