10. TLA+ skeleton
This skeleton states the verification obligation for a deployment. It has not yet been run through a model checker. Preservation quantifies over all of I, so it needs a symbolic checker such as Apalache. TLC can check Safety on a small instance.
------------------------------ MODULE STT1024_A4_State ------------------------------
EXTENDS Naturals, Sequences, FiniteSets

CONSTANTS
    M, SAFE,              \* finite set of FSM modes, and the safe mode
    T, TaskID,            \* finite sets: tiles, task ids
    Sensors,              \* finite set of declared sensors
    Domain,               \* [Sensors -> SUBSET Nat], each finite
    FaultVal,             \* [Sensors -> Nat], the reserved fault value
    Entry,                \* finite set of log-window entries
    W, MaxCycle,          \* memory window size, cycle bound
    Init0,                \* set of initial states
    TCycle, AET, TReact,  \* timing, in the same unit
    Delta(_, _),          \* deployment-supplied transition
    PhiInt(_),            \* internal invariant over a state
    PhiWorld(_)           \* world-safety predicate over a state

ASSUME /\ IsFiniteSet(M) /\ SAFE \in M
       /\ IsFiniteSet(T) /\ IsFiniteSet(TaskID) /\ IsFiniteSet(Sensors)
       /\ IsFiniteSet(Entry) /\ W \in Nat /\ MaxCycle \in Nat
       /\ \A s \in Sensors : IsFiniteSet(Domain[s]) /\ FaultVal[s] \in Domain[s]
       /\ TCycle + AET <= TReact

BSeq(S, n) == UNION { [1..k -> S] : k \in 0..n }

WorldSet == { f \in [Sensors -> UNION { Domain[s] : s \in Sensors }] :
                \A s \in Sensors : f[s] \in Domain[s] }

State == [ mode  : M,
           tile  : T,
           task  : TaskID,
           world : WorldSet,
           mem   : BSeq(Entry, W),
           cycle : 0..MaxCycle ]

Sigma == WorldSet                      \* one reading per sensor per cycle (§4.1)

InvSet == { s \in State : PhiInt(s) /\ (PhiWorld(s) \/ s.mode = SAFE) }

SafeStep(s, sg) == [s EXCEPT !.mode = SAFE, !.world = sg]

\* Obligations on the deployment-supplied operators
ASSUME DeltaTotal  == \A s \in State, sg \in Sigma : Delta(s, sg) \in State
ASSUME SafeClosure == \A s \in InvSet, sg \in Sigma : PhiInt(SafeStep(s, sg))

VARIABLE st

Init == st \in Init0
Next == \E sg \in Sigma : st' = Delta(st, sg)
Spec == Init /\ [][Next]_st

InitInInv    == Init0 \subseteq InvSet
Preservation == \A s \in InvSet, sg \in Sigma : Delta(s, sg) \in InvSet
Safety       == [](st \in InvSet)

THEOREM Spec => Safety      \* follows from InitInInv and Preservation
=======================================================================================
The theorem follows from InitInInv and Preservation by induction on the run. Those two are what must be checked for a concrete deployment.
11. Conclusion
A4 defines the state representation and world interface that the agentic extension requires. Without it the Verifiable Agency Theorem has no premises, VERIFY has no object, and the log has no content.
The commitments:
1.  State is finite, by construction.
2.  State is deterministic: transitions are functions, not relations.
3.  State is total: faults and doubt are values, not absences.
4.  Unsafe transitions are not committed: the agent enters SAFE.
5.  The guarantees are relative to the model, and the model's gaps are declared.
A3 sits on top of A4. A5 checks predicates over A4 state. A7 logs A4 transitions.
Appendix A: Plain language
What is the state of an AI agent? Everything it knows about itself and the world, right now, in one place.
Why does it have to be finite? So you can prove what it will do next.
How do you make it finite? You quantize the world. Sensors have a fixed set of values. Numbers have bounds.
But the world is infinite. The world is. The agent's model of it is not. The model is a finite projection: enough for the task, and no more.
Can the agent be unsure? About the world, yes: a reading can be marked faulty, stale or implausible, and the agent treats that as a fact. What it cannot be is in an undefined state.
What if the world goes somewhere dangerous? The agent cannot stop the world. It moves to a safe mode, refuses to act, and logs it.
Is that safe? Safe relative to the model. If the model leaves out a hazard, or a sensor lies convincingly, no proof built on the model can see it. That is why every deployment lists its hazards and the ones it has not covered.
What is this called? A4: the state and world model.
